+# Independent full review: conjugation-invariant norm dichotomies
+
+PASS for all five scoped results. The original finitely presented counterexample question remains unsolved after five substantive turns. No mandatory mathematical correction was found. This is an AI-assisted audit, not external peer review or historical novelty certification.
+
+The immutable 36-file author packet is bound by FINAL_AUTHOR_MANIFEST SHA256 db357e6334b49123b461dfd762f5fc05082997692ca33ce1ae4a898d16694ec8 and WIP a4630abefcf559b3a61b5c5c6b57b409fcfe26a2.
+
+## Exact source and scope
+
+The original Kędra contribution and printed 199 question were checked, including visual inspection. Finite presentation is essential; the two alternatives concern bounded versus linearly growing cyclic powers, and bounded versus detection by an ordinary homogeneous real quasimorphism. The later partial-quasimorphism theorem has a different defect condition and is not an answer to this question. Finite ordinary generation supplies compatible finite normal generating norms, whose choice does not affect the two dichotomies up to Lipschitz constants.
+
+The cited BGKM solvable theorem explicitly requires a finitely generated nilpotent commutator subgroup. That hypothesis is not presumed for every finitely generated metabelian group. The packet's direct compression proof therefore does genuine work within its stated class while retaining credit and declining novelty certification.
+
+## Turn 1: abelian normal compression and metabelian groups
+
+The right-suffix telescoping formula expresses each conjugation difference as a sum of the fixed generator-image subgroups even when the action operators do not commute. Combining terms is justified inside the abelian normal subgroup. Every resulting commutator has a fixed generator on one side, giving the claimed norm bound independently of the other entry's size.
+
+After quotienting by [G,G'], the derived image is central and generated by the finitely many basic commutators. Their integer powers are represented by commutators with a fixed side, and the residual augmentation term is uniformly bounded. This proves the d(d+1) bound without assuming finite generation of G'. Lifting shortest abelianization words gives the metric comparison. Torsion images yield bounded powers; infinite-order images admit real character detectors. The homogeneous-quasimorphism argument uses conjugacy invariance, its fixed defect and homogeneity correctly, including descent through bounded cosets.
+
+## Turn 2: finite extensions
+
+The bounded-kernel lifting statement concerns the ambient norm, not the subgroup's intrinsic norm. The core of a finite-index metabelian subgroup remains normal, finite index, finitely generated and metabelian. Its derived subgroup is bounded in the ambient norm by the first theorem.
+
+The virtually abelian quotient argument is sound. In the central finite-index quotient, finitely many conjugates of basic commutators generate the derived subgroup. Its central intersection is finitely generated by Schreier. The transfer is a homomorphism and restricts to the index power map on the center, so that intersection has finite exponent and is finite. The derived subgroup is therefore finite. Lifting through the bounded kernels proves the virtually metabelian conclusion. The dihedral example accurately demonstrates collapse of an intrinsic subgroup metric and does not contradict the theorem.
+
+## Turn 3: split abelian extensions
+
+The coinvariant quotient is exactly M_Q times Q because the extension is split. Its abelian factor is finitely generated as a quotient factor, with no assumption that M is finitely generated. The kernel diameter and additive norm comparison are correct. For product generators the product norm is the sum of the factor norms; changing finite normal generating sets only changes constants. Both dichotomies transfer through this product and bounded kernel, with detectors pulled back as ordinary homogeneous quasimorphisms. The nonsplit Heisenberg warning identifies precisely the missing direct-product inference and is not mislabeled a counterexample.
+
+## Turn 4: effective stable norms on a promised class
+
+The relation lattice is the correct abelianization lattice. The real L1 relaxation gives a lower bound for every integer multiple, while rational attainment and denominator clearing yield equality on one arithmetic progression. Subadditivity and bounded residue classes prove the full limit. The bounded-derived hypothesis transfers that limit to the normal word norm without needing an effective diameter bound.
+
+The compact dual polytope, rational primal-dual equality and exhaustive independent active-coordinate constraints give a terminating exact algorithm, including rank-deficient relation matrices and torsion. Vanishing of the value is equivalent to a torsion abelianization image under the supplied hypothesis. No recognition algorithm for that hypothesis is claimed. The rational cyclic example, Heisenberg example and free-group caution all have the stated scope.
+
+## Turn 5: finite braid truncations versus the shift limit
+
+The conjugation identity for delta is used only in its valid nonwrapping range. For N=K+2 it checks every finite presentation relator and produces all standard braid generators, hence a surjection. Injectivity of the local braid subgroup is unnecessary. The proposed word maps to exactly the credited braid alpha.
+
+I visually checked Brandenbursky–Kędra printed 2883 and the preceding definition. The ordinary homogenized signature is -2 on this alpha for every relevant strand number. Pullback therefore detects the word in each finite truncation, with finite constants allowed to depend on K. No conclusion about every other element is inferred.
+
+In the infinite-relator group, the local subgroup is an image of B3 and the word belongs to its derived subgroup. Conjugation by t cubed separates all the two-index supports by at least two, so one fixed element strongly displaces every required finite family. The exact BIP definition and Theorem 2.2(i), together with the seven-displacement-commutator proof of its norm inequality, were checked. Its displacement energy is at most the norm of t cubed; applying it at each element's actual finite commutator length gives the uniform bound 42. The identity case is separate. The result does not assert non-finite-presentability of the displayed infinite-relator group or preserve the metric of the original infinite braid example.
+
+## Reproduction and disposition
+
+All 345,888 author assertions replay byte-exact, with 62 public manifest entries and eight source bindings verified. The independent checker adds 47,964 exact controls for genuinely noncommuting action telescoping, Heisenberg identities, rational stable-norm certificates and braid support separation. Both mathematical replays use standard-library Python. The signature and displacement results remain explicitly credited primary inputs; finite checks do not recompute knot signatures or prove infinite group statements by enumeration.
